===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*terkʷ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|trusse}}, from {{der|en|fro|trousse}}, {{m|fro|torse}}. {{doublet|en|torse|trousse}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|hernia}} A [[bandage]] and [[belt]] used to hold a [[hernia]] in place.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Lippincott Williams & Wilkins| title=Professional Guide to Diseases| chapter=4| isbn=0781778999| page=280| passage=A '''truss''' may keep the abdominal contents from protruding into the hernial sac; however, this won't cure the hernia.}}
# {{lb|en|architecture}} A [[structure]] made up of one or more [[triangular]] units made from straight [[beam]]s of wood or metal, which is used to support a structure as in a [[roof]] or [[bridge]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1952|month=September|title=Chepstow River Bridge Centenary|journal=Railway Magazine|page=623|text=The weight of the wrought-ironwork in each of the '''trusses''' is 460 tons, inclusive of the longitudinal and cross girders weighing 130 tons.}}
# {{lb|en|architecture}} A [[triangular]] [[bracket]].
# An old English farming [[measurement]]. One truss of straw equalled 36 pounds, a truss of old hay equalled 56 pounds, a truss of new hay equalled 60 pounds, and 36 trusses equalled one load.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A bundle; a package.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Shepheardes Calender|May|year=1586
|passage=bearing a '''truss''' of trifles at his back}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} A padded jacket or dress worn under armour, to protect the body from the effects of [[friction]].
#* {{RQ:Drayton Poly-Olbion
|passage=Puts off his palmer's weed unto his '''truss''', which bore / The stains of ancient arms.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} Part of a woman's dress; a [[stomacher]].
# {{lb|en|botany}} A [[tuft]] of [[flower]]s or [[cluster]] of [[fruit]]s formed at the top of the main [[stem]] of certain plants.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The rope or iron used to keep the centre of a [[yard]] to the [[mast]].

